a Massachusetts woman was sentenced to  15 months in jail for using text  messages to encourage her friend to  commit suicide a judge ruled that  Michelle Carter will not have to go to  jail until all of her state appeals are  exhausted she was convicted of  involuntary manslaughter in the death of  Conrad Roy in June 48 hours  correspondent Erin Moriarty spoke with  Roy's family after yesterday's  sentencing hearing it's an interview  you'll see only on CBS this morning  Erin good morning good morning Michele  Carter was allowed to go home last night  with her family after she was sentenced  if her appeals are successful she may  never serve a day behind bars her  punishment left Conrad Roy's loved ones  shell-shocked  and at a loss for words this part of the  case is over it's not for all of us no  it never will be  does this make it any easier though that  you know we want to put it past us we  want to move on the best way we can  Conrad Roy's family was distraught as it  became clear they would not see Michelle  Carter called off to jail there's  nothing she could say that could make up  for what she did no Carter was on the  phone with Roy as he was dying of carbon  monoxide poisoning inside his truck in  July 2014 she never called for help when  Roy apparently changed his mind and got  out of his vehicle she even told him to  get back inside  Roy suffered from depression the hardest  thing for me is to be comfortable in my  own skin and before he killed himself  Carter sent the 18 year old dozens of  texts pressuring him to commit suicide  one read hang yourself jump off a  building stab yourself I don't know  there's a lot of ways Michelle Carter  exploited my son's weaknesses and used  him as a pawn where was her humanity  Carter who herself has struggled with  anxiety and eating disorders wept at  times during the hearing this is a  tragedy for two families judge Lawrence  Moniz sentenced her to two and a half  years in jail but he said Carter would  only have to spend half of that 15  months behind bars she also received  five years probation the judge noted  Carter was three weeks shy of turning 18  at the time of Roy's suicide still a  child in the eyes of the law  the fact that they are still of that  young age off is a greater promise of  rehabilitation judge Moniz then put  Carter's sentence on hold to allow her  lawyers to file Appeals prosecutors were  disappointed they had asked for at least  a seven years sentence and said that  Michelle Carter needs to be held  accountable  Conrad Roy's mother told me last night  that she hopes her son's death will lead  to a new law so that no other mother has  to go through her pain she may be the  most difficult person I've ever had to  talk to you feel she that sentencing did  not you know that word closure did not  help this family it was very very  difficult it's still horrifying to read  these messages every single time you  look at them I can't imagine what it's  like for the family no and you do get  the sense in the court I was surprised  that Michelle Carter didn't actually do  a statement for the judge right and  admitting she recognized what she had  done great work on this and tomorrow  night on 48 hours an in-depth look at  the case the trial and the sentencing 48  hours death by text airs Saturday at  10:00 9:00 central
 

